-    // Athena start - stop offering every entity in the world to every player that moved.
-    //
-    // The second half of this method used to walk every tracked entity once per player that moved
-    // four blocks, asking each whether that player was within its tracking box. On a populated
-    // world that is (entities) x (players moving) box tests per tick for a result that only ever
-    // names the entities standing near the player. The walk cannot simply be narrowed, because it
-    // was doing two different jobs at once:
-    //
-    //  - telling a player about an entity that came into range, and
-    //  - taking away an entity the player walked away from.
-    //
-    // Only the first is a question about what is near the player; the second is a question about
-    // what the player already has, and the entity in it is by definition out of range, so no
-    // spatial lookup can find it. They are split here. The removal half rides along on the pass
-    // over every entry that this method already makes - each entry offers itself to the players it
-    // is already sending to, which costs nothing for the entries nobody is watching. The addition
-    // half then walks the chunks around the player, which is where the entities that could newly
-    // come into range have to be.
-    //
-    // What the chunk walk can miss, and why no client can tell:
-    //  - an entity whose chunk is not loaded. updatePlayer would only have added it if
-    //    tracker.attachedToPlayer were set, since isPlayerWatchingThisChunk is false for a chunk
-    //    the player does not have. A player keeps the chunks within their view distance loaded,
-    //    and no tracking range here exceeds it, so an entity close enough to be added is in a
-    //    loaded chunk.
-    //  - an entity whose chunkCoordX/Z still point at a chunk it has left, which is the same gap
-    //    the per-chunk tracker update already accepts: it is in no slice for that moment, and it
-    //    is re-evaluated as soon as it or the player moves four blocks.
-    //
-    // Player entries are kept out of the chunk walk's radius: they track at 512 blocks (clamped to
-    // the view distance), which alone would have set the radius for everything. There are at most
-    // max-players of them, so they are offered directly instead.
     public void updatePlayers() {
-        List<EntityHuman> players = this.world.players;
